{-
   What `deriving` refuses on a qualified alias - Analysis-Derive.md §3.

   Every case here is a condition the forwarding rewrite is well-defined under, reported at the class
   name in the clause rather than from inside an expansion - which is the whole of what §3's "where
   errors land" section asks for.
-}

-- The content has no instance to forward to. This is the common failure, and it names both halves of
-- the head the derivation would have needed.
alias qualified Named = String deriving (Bitwise)

-- The class variable does not occur bare. `fromValue(number: I64) -> Maybe(a)` puts it inside
-- another type, and there is no coercion from `Maybe(Ticket)` to `Maybe(I64)` for a forwarding body
-- to emit - Analysis-Derive.md §2's sixth point, which is Haskell's `GeneralizedNewtypeDeriving`
-- restriction written down as a condition.
alias qualified Ticket = I64 deriving (Enum)

-- A class with more than one type variable. There is no single "the content" to forward to.
alias qualified Bits = I64 deriving (Bitcast)

-- The class is not one.
alias qualified Score = Int deriving (Nonexistent)

-- Structurally decided, so there is nothing an instance could say - the same refusal a written
-- `instance TrivialCopy(T)` gets.
alias qualified Token = Int deriving (TrivialCopy)

-- A generic newtype, whose head would need the context worked out from its content.
alias qualified Wrapped(a) = a deriving (Eq)

-- Derived and written for one class and one type. Reported at the second of the two, which is the
-- clause here because the sweep takes declarations in order.
alias qualified Flags = I64 deriving (Truth)
instance Truth(Flags):
    fn truthy(value: Flags) -> Bool = True
